WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.184 --> 00:00:01.624
(bird calls)

00:00:01.624 --> 00:00:04.374
(dramatic music)

00:00:17.768 --> 00:00:20.015
- [Host] Mankind and the elements.

00:00:20.015 --> 00:00:22.942
For some, it's an uncomfortable bond.

00:00:22.942 --> 00:00:23.897
- Water's very powerful.

00:00:23.897 --> 00:00:24.771
It's very deceiving

00:00:24.771 --> 00:00:27.066
and you have to have great respect for it.

00:00:27.066 --> 00:00:29.593
- [Host] For others, when weather strikes,

00:00:29.593 --> 00:00:31.484
inspiration begins.

00:00:31.484 --> 00:00:33.696
- We're just nine and 11 year old girls

00:00:33.696 --> 00:00:35.657
who did a science project.

00:00:35.657 --> 00:00:38.743
- [Host] These are the
people who challenge nature,

00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:42.076
seek out its limits, reveal its secrets,

00:00:43.292 --> 00:00:46.419
and embrace its awesome power.

00:00:46.419 --> 00:00:49.253
In this episode, we'll meet two sisters

00:00:49.253 --> 00:00:51.110
whose ambitious science experiment

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.925
took them up and out of this world.

00:00:53.925 --> 00:00:56.147
- [Girl] We hope that
our projects teach them

00:00:56.147 --> 00:00:57.722
that they can do whatever they want

00:00:57.722 --> 00:00:59.303
if they put their heart into it.

00:00:59.303 --> 00:01:01.310
- [Host] A woman climbing
to outrageous heights

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.017
in order to clean the
nation's wind turbines.

00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:07.335
- You're basically doing
construction work on ropes.

00:01:07.335 --> 00:01:09.653
- [Host] And a photographer
wading in the icy waters

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:13.319
of the Great Lakes to shoot
what he calls liquid mountains.

00:01:13.319 --> 00:01:17.662
- Where I'm at, it's more
like a giant washing machine.

00:01:17.662 --> 00:01:19.952
- [Host] These pioneers of
the great outdoors ahead

00:01:19.952 --> 00:01:21.452
on That's Amazing.

00:01:28.337 --> 00:01:31.087
(dramatic music)

00:01:37.971 --> 00:01:40.054
When you think of the Great Lakes,

00:01:40.054 --> 00:01:42.054
you may not expect this.

00:01:43.931 --> 00:01:47.352
These photographs of
magnificent towering waves

00:01:47.352 --> 00:01:50.733
weren't taken in the
Pacific or the Atlantic.

00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:53.235
They were taken at Lake Erie.

00:01:53.235 --> 00:01:55.654
- The thing that I find
amazes a lot of people

00:01:55.654 --> 00:01:59.365
that aren't familiar with them
is the sheer size of them.

00:01:59.365 --> 00:02:00.865
I've been with lots of people that

00:02:00.865 --> 00:02:02.242
for the first time when
they're seeing them,

00:02:02.242 --> 00:02:03.075
they're like, “Really?

00:02:03.075 --> 00:02:04.352
This isn't an ocean?”

00:02:04.352 --> 00:02:06.581
- [Host] Dave Sandford is
a professional photographer

00:02:06.581 --> 00:02:08.918
from Ontario, Canada.

00:02:08.918 --> 00:02:10.131
To do his work,

00:02:10.131 --> 00:02:13.888
Sandford waits for the
perfect weather conditions,

00:02:13.888 --> 00:02:16.801
heading out into fierce
winds and ice cold water

00:02:16.801 --> 00:02:18.884
to make his masterpieces.

00:02:26.687 --> 00:02:29.437
(dramatic music)

00:02:35.406 --> 00:02:39.029
- Lake Erie is in the most
southern part of Ontario.

00:02:39.029 --> 00:02:41.536
It's the smallest of the Great Lakes.

00:02:41.536 --> 00:02:45.703
As far as the depth goes,
it's the shallowest by far.

00:02:46.747 --> 00:02:48.414
- Being a large lake,

00:02:48.414 --> 00:02:52.504
but relatively shallow compared
to the other Great Lakes,

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:55.087
creates unique wave conditions.

00:02:56.302 --> 00:03:00.219
The waves on Lake Erie
tend to be closer together

00:03:00.219 --> 00:03:03.765
with a lot more of the violence
on the white water on top.

00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:04.848
- There's a lot of people

00:03:04.848 --> 00:03:06.558
that have lost their lives on Lake Erie.

00:03:06.558 --> 00:03:09.729
There's been estimated
somewhere over 8,000 ship wrecks

00:03:09.729 --> 00:03:11.438
on Lake Erie over the years.

00:03:11.438 --> 00:03:13.898
- At her worst, Lake
Erie can be murderous.

00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:17.526
In fact, when she's murderous,
we call her a widow-maker.

00:03:17.526 --> 00:03:20.109
(upbeat music)

00:03:23.158 --> 00:03:24.784
- To be a nature photographer,

00:03:24.784 --> 00:03:26.265
I think that one of the number one things

00:03:26.265 --> 00:03:28.452
you have to have is patience.

00:03:28.452 --> 00:03:32.084
The first wave photo that
I posted from Lake Erie,

00:03:32.084 --> 00:03:34.374
I came home that night
and I posted an image

00:03:34.374 --> 00:03:35.624
and right away,

00:03:36.795 --> 00:03:38.881
the number of comments on it,

00:03:38.881 --> 00:03:42.432
it was crazy compared to
what I normally would get.

00:03:42.432 --> 00:03:46.599
And the number of likes. (laughs)

00:03:50.935 --> 00:03:52.394
If I know I'm going in the water,

00:03:52.394 --> 00:03:55.857
it starts from like the moment
I wake up in the morning.

00:03:55.857 --> 00:03:58.235
There's a lot of mental preparation.

00:03:58.235 --> 00:04:00.567
It is an adrenaline rush too, for sure.

00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:03.527
Like, I have no idea how things
are going to unfold.

00:04:03.527 --> 00:04:05.251
It's before the sun's even coming up

00:04:05.251 --> 00:04:09.121
and you can see the water in the distance

00:04:09.121 --> 00:04:12.834
and those waves exploding into the air

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:15.417
and it's just such a cool rush.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:20.341
The window of opportunity to
photograph the lakes for me

00:04:20.341 --> 00:04:24.508
is generally from maybe
mid-October through early December.

00:04:25.596 --> 00:04:26.866
It's the best time of the year

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:29.144
when you still have that warm air masses

00:04:29.144 --> 00:04:30.642
that are around this region

00:04:30.642 --> 00:04:32.812
and then those cold air
masses that are coming

00:04:32.812 --> 00:04:36.979
from up north that give you
these really prime conditions.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.912
Obviously weather changes
and it's constantly changing.

00:04:44.912 --> 00:04:48.074
The biggest factor is wind
speed and wind direction.

00:04:48.074 --> 00:04:50.243
A southwest wind is the best.

00:04:50.243 --> 00:04:52.288
And minimal wind speeds,

00:04:52.288 --> 00:04:55.671
I'm usually looking at
around 30 miles an hour

00:04:55.671 --> 00:04:57.211
at the bare minimum.

00:04:57.211 --> 00:04:59.848
The high end of it,
um, you're actually getting

00:04:59.848 --> 00:05:02.894
the category one hurricane level winds,

00:05:02.894 --> 00:05:04.392
over 70 miles an hour.

00:05:04.392 --> 00:05:07.384
It's a crazy mass of water

00:05:07.384 --> 00:05:09.764
and that's when you're getting these waves

00:05:09.764 --> 00:05:12.097
that are upwards of 30 feet.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.944
The tough thing about lake waves is

00:05:18.944 --> 00:05:20.112
it's not like ocean waves

00:05:20.112 --> 00:05:22.363
where you have sets that come in

00:05:22.363 --> 00:05:23.969
and then there's a bit of a lull.

00:05:23.969 --> 00:05:26.575
Lake waves are constantly pounding.

00:05:26.575 --> 00:05:28.618
You know, you virtually have like two seconds,

00:05:28.618 --> 00:05:30.910
two and a half seconds between each wave.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:35.905
And where I'm at, it's more
like a giant washing machine.

00:05:35.905 --> 00:05:37.259
It's not easy.

00:05:37.259 --> 00:05:39.546
You're kind of cold after a while too.

00:05:39.546 --> 00:05:42.298
That water's only around
the 50 degree mark.

00:05:42.298 --> 00:05:44.507
There's days where
there's ice in the water

00:05:44.507 --> 00:05:48.481
and you're literally like what am I doing?

00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:49.389
Water is very powerful.

00:05:49.389 --> 00:05:50.307
It's very deceiving

00:05:50.307 --> 00:05:52.769
and you have to have great respect for it.

00:05:52.769 --> 00:05:55.979
You can get pulled under and
pulled out with a rip current.

00:05:55.979 --> 00:05:58.648
It's obviously a lot
stronger than you are.

00:05:58.648 --> 00:06:03.342
You have to know your limitations
and know when to say when.

00:06:03.342 --> 00:06:06.322
(piano music)

00:06:06.322 --> 00:06:07.614
The waves that are generated

00:06:07.614 --> 00:06:10.536
from this refraction off the pier,

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:13.244
those are the waves
that I'm photographing.

00:06:13.244 --> 00:06:15.999
Where you have, you know, two masses of water,

00:06:15.999 --> 00:06:18.416
two waves that are moving
in opposite directions

00:06:18.416 --> 00:06:22.303
that are meeting and
colliding and they actually,

00:06:22.303 --> 00:06:25.089
they'll hit and they twist and they turn,

00:06:25.089 --> 00:06:28.006
and it's a phenomenal thing to see.

00:06:29.176 --> 00:06:32.222
It's just building this
massive liquid mountain

00:06:32.222 --> 00:06:37.183
that literally lasts all
of like a second, if that.

00:06:37.183 --> 00:06:40.816
Sometimes it's a fraction
of a moment in time.

00:06:40.816 --> 00:06:42.151
And that's one of the things that I love

00:06:42.151 --> 00:06:45.611
is the challenge of capturing that moment.

00:06:45.611 --> 00:06:47.653
- [Host] Dave Sandford's
liquid mountain photographs

00:06:47.653 --> 00:06:50.696
have appeared in several
online publications,

00:06:50.696 --> 00:06:54.022
including the Washington Post.

00:06:54.022 --> 00:06:57.244
He also sells prints on his website.

00:06:57.244 --> 00:06:59.181
- I guess one of the
things that I really hope

00:06:59.181 --> 00:07:02.264
is that my photos can allow people to

00:07:04.341 --> 00:07:08.261
appreciate what they have
as far as nature goes

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.011
and learn to love it.

00:07:10.851 --> 00:07:14.601
And cherish it and
embrace it and protect it.

00:07:19.271 --> 00:07:21.688
(calm music)

00:07:27.653 --> 00:07:30.075
- It's not very many people really know,

00:07:30.075 --> 00:07:34.035
but the Great Lakes has
thousands of ship wrecks.

00:07:34.035 --> 00:07:35.371
In Wisconsin, for sure,

00:07:35.371 --> 00:07:39.288
we know we have 750, um,
historic ship wreck losses.

00:07:42.715 --> 00:07:45.128
My name is Caitlin Zant and
I'm a maritime archeologist

00:07:45.128 --> 00:07:48.673
with the Wisconsin Historical Society.

00:07:48.673 --> 00:07:49.624
(water splashes)

00:07:49.624 --> 00:07:51.931
I would say the main goal of what we do

00:07:51.931 --> 00:07:55.638
in maritime archeology is to
preserve our maritime history

00:07:55.638 --> 00:07:58.936
and also the history of the
United States in general.

00:07:58.936 --> 00:08:01.185
The ships are quite sturdy,

00:08:01.185 --> 00:08:03.440
but they've also been under water for

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.190
up to 100, 150 years.

00:08:07.194 --> 00:08:09.066
Our ship wrecks don't disappear over time

00:08:09.066 --> 00:08:11.241
like they do in the oceans.

00:08:11.241 --> 00:08:12.992
The fresh water that's very cold

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:14.322
actually does a really good job

00:08:14.322 --> 00:08:16.953
at preserving these ship wrecks.

00:08:16.953 --> 00:08:18.936
One of the really great
things about Lake Michigan

00:08:18.936 --> 00:08:20.457
that makes it really unique

00:08:20.457 --> 00:08:24.207
is the fact that you do
have fresh clear water.

00:08:24.207 --> 00:08:27.130
You can see, many times,
especially on deep wrecks,

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:28.463
100 to 150 feet.

00:08:29.674 --> 00:08:31.343
When you are down there
looking at a ship wreck,

00:08:31.343 --> 00:08:34.299
you start to think of all
the sailors that sailed on it

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:35.466
or if it was a passenger ship,

00:08:35.466 --> 00:08:37.819
the passengers that happened to be on it.

00:08:37.819 --> 00:08:42.143
You can still find pieces of
clothing in certain areas,

00:08:42.143 --> 00:08:44.061
buttons, shoes.

00:08:44.061 --> 00:08:45.859
And so that really gives insight

00:08:45.859 --> 00:08:48.648
to the lives of these sailors.

00:08:48.648 --> 00:08:52.026
There's definitely a little
bit of a thrill of discovery.

00:08:52.026 --> 00:08:56.076
When we go down on a ship,
even on a ship that we know,

00:08:56.076 --> 00:08:57.323
we think we know what it is,

00:08:57.323 --> 00:08:59.700
but there's still always that little

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:01.621
sense of excitement that
you're gonna find something

00:09:01.621 --> 00:09:04.371
that no one else has seen before.

00:09:07.626 --> 00:09:10.376
(dramatic music)

00:09:14.051 --> 00:09:15.301
- [Host] You're likely to spot them

00:09:15.301 --> 00:09:17.801
only in the most remote of places,

00:09:17.801 --> 00:09:20.516
where the wind blows fiercest.

00:09:20.516 --> 00:09:22.372
Giant wind turbines.

00:09:22.372 --> 00:09:24.022
They're increasingly
supplying more and more

00:09:24.022 --> 00:09:25.559
of our clean energy.

00:09:25.559 --> 00:09:29.776
And when they break down,
they need to be fixed fast.

00:09:29.776 --> 00:09:33.735
It's a job only a few people
are equipped to handle.

00:09:33.735 --> 00:09:37.368
Those who are afraid of
heights need not apply.

00:09:37.368 --> 00:09:40.658
- You're basically doing
construction work on ropes.

00:09:40.658 --> 00:09:43.997
Most people think, G”od,
you're insane.” (laughs)

00:09:43.997 --> 00:09:45.708
That that's totally dangerous.

00:09:45.708 --> 00:09:49.503
But actually, it's statistically
safer than driving to work.

00:09:49.503 --> 00:09:52.084
When you're driving past
wind turbines on the highway,

00:09:52.084 --> 00:09:54.255
you may never know that there's
somebody up there dangling,

00:09:54.255 --> 00:09:55.966
but next time, you should look up there

00:09:55.966 --> 00:09:58.049
because you might see me.

00:10:10.104 --> 00:10:13.152
- [Host] Jessica Kilroy is a rock climber.

00:10:13.152 --> 00:10:16.156
But she makes her living as
a wind turbine technician,

00:10:16.156 --> 00:10:17.945
repairing damaged turbine blades

00:10:17.945 --> 00:10:21.072
on towers that are as tall as 350 feet.

00:10:21.072 --> 00:10:23.006
- It's easy to do this job safely.

00:10:23.006 --> 00:10:25.327
You just have to do it carefully.

00:10:25.327 --> 00:10:27.757
I'm a rock climber and it's obvious

00:10:27.757 --> 00:10:29.584
that when you're rock
climbing all the time,

00:10:29.584 --> 00:10:30.792
you're not making any money.

00:10:30.792 --> 00:10:31.793
You end up living in your van

00:10:31.793 --> 00:10:34.628
and wondering how you're
gonna pay the bills.

00:10:34.628 --> 00:10:37.300
Rock climbing and blade repair
require the same skills.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:40.346
Being able to have no fear of heights,

00:10:40.346 --> 00:10:43.390
be comfortable on ropes
as well as being able to

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:47.557
figure out how to get yourself
out of a weird situation.

00:10:49.895 --> 00:10:51.984
- [Host] Here in Plymouth, Massachusetts,

00:10:51.984 --> 00:10:54.649
the average wind speed
is 20 miles per hour.

00:10:54.649 --> 00:10:56.507
But these turbines can easily operate

00:10:56.507 --> 00:10:58.924
at winds twice that strength.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:03.702
Since wind farms are
built in remote areas,

00:11:03.702 --> 00:11:05.287
the turbine towers themselves

00:11:05.287 --> 00:11:08.212
are often the tallest
structures for miles.

00:11:08.212 --> 00:11:10.889
This can make them perfect lightning rods.

00:11:10.889 --> 00:11:12.249
(thunder booms)

00:11:12.249 --> 00:11:13.711
Towers have lightning protection systems

00:11:13.711 --> 00:11:15.505
that ground electricity.

00:11:15.505 --> 00:11:18.215
But sometimes, lightning
exits out of the blade,

00:11:18.215 --> 00:11:20.760
damaging its fiberglass exterior.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:22.928
Blades can also crack
due to weather erosion

00:11:22.928 --> 00:11:25.347
from dust, rain, snow and ice.

00:11:25.347 --> 00:11:28.684
Exposing its wooden interior
to corrosive moisture.

00:11:28.684 --> 00:11:31.478
If left unchecked, the blades will weaken

00:11:31.478 --> 00:11:33.505
and eventually break off the tower.

00:11:33.505 --> 00:11:34.828
- That's the main problem.

00:11:34.828 --> 00:11:36.879
And we don't want blades
just breaking off.

00:11:36.879 --> 00:11:40.238
That's usually why we repair
them as soon as possible.

00:11:40.238 --> 00:11:42.491
Usually you see like a small dark spot

00:11:42.491 --> 00:11:43.785
and that's where lightning has struck

00:11:43.785 --> 00:11:46.744
and then you can grind
into it and look further

00:11:46.744 --> 00:11:48.161
and see how deep it goes.

00:11:48.161 --> 00:11:51.627
We repair the fiberglass
using sanders and grinders

00:11:51.627 --> 00:11:54.794
and crowbars or whatever you
need to get the job done.

00:11:54.794 --> 00:11:57.169
It's really similar to surfboard repair,

00:11:57.169 --> 00:12:00.847
except there's 350 feet
of nothingness beneath me.

00:12:00.847 --> 00:12:02.720
You're on the blade and you straddle it

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:05.553
and the wind is bucking you
around and you're on a bull.

00:12:05.553 --> 00:12:07.803
Feel like I'm in the rodeo.

00:12:09.849 --> 00:12:11.976
I think it's easy for people
to look at me and think

00:12:11.976 --> 00:12:15.272
blonde chick, climber, life
must come easy for her.

00:12:15.272 --> 00:12:18.317
But I've had to fight for this life.

00:12:18.317 --> 00:12:22.487
I was born with my bones
not aligned correctly.

00:12:22.487 --> 00:12:25.241
I had to wear straight leg
braces up until high school.

00:12:25.241 --> 00:12:27.952
The doctors said I could
not do so many things.

00:12:27.952 --> 00:12:29.703
They said you can't do
this, you can't do that.

00:12:29.703 --> 00:12:31.638
And, like, my dad is a climber.

00:12:31.638 --> 00:12:33.378
Back in the ‘80s and the ‘90s,

00:12:33.378 --> 00:12:36.502
my dad and his friends
were out climbing big walls

00:12:36.502 --> 00:12:38.460
and putting up first
ascents in the Tetons.

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:39.837
And I was just like, dude,

00:12:39.837 --> 00:12:42.585
you guys are the coolest ever
and I want to be you guys.

00:12:42.585 --> 00:12:44.260
I had so many years as a kid

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:46.181
of just looking at climbing magazines

00:12:46.181 --> 00:12:48.804
and watching my friends
running around outside,

00:12:48.804 --> 00:12:51.181
jump roping, playing basketball,

00:12:51.181 --> 00:12:54.480
doing all the things that I wanted to do.

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:56.770
(exhales)

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:58.023
That part's hard.

00:12:58.023 --> 00:12:59.523
Sorry.

00:12:59.523 --> 00:13:02.356
(breathes deeply)

00:13:05.785 --> 00:13:08.888
I decided I'm not gonna let
people's limits be my limits.

00:13:08.888 --> 00:13:11.620
And I decided to go so much further past

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:14.870
what I even thought my own limits were.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:23.503
The first thing I
usually do is harness up.

00:13:25.384 --> 00:13:26.842
There's a ladder in the inside.

00:13:26.842 --> 00:13:28.535
It's 350 feet tall

00:13:28.535 --> 00:13:31.725
and you just climb every
rung of that ladder

00:13:31.725 --> 00:13:33.558
all the way up to the top.

00:13:33.558 --> 00:13:37.648
The view from the top is
expansive and just awesome.

00:13:37.648 --> 00:13:39.065
You're the tallest thing around.

00:13:39.065 --> 00:13:42.070
You can get butterflies in
your stomach a little bit.

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:44.151
There is outside risk with this job.

00:13:44.151 --> 00:13:46.026
There's adverse weather conditions,

00:13:46.026 --> 00:13:48.119
as well as forest fires.

00:13:48.119 --> 00:13:49.616
This summer, I was on a wind turbine

00:13:49.616 --> 00:13:52.532
where a wildfire just
came rolling through.

00:13:52.532 --> 00:13:53.966
We bailed really quick.

00:13:53.966 --> 00:13:56.746
And when we were out, you know, driving away,

00:13:56.746 --> 00:13:58.417
I have a picture out the window

00:13:58.417 --> 00:14:02.221
and the wind turbine was just
like swept up in the fire.

00:14:02.221 --> 00:14:04.638
(calm music)

00:14:07.749 --> 00:14:10.597
I feel blessed to be able to be

00:14:10.597 --> 00:14:12.887
in some of the most beautiful
places in the world,

00:14:12.887 --> 00:14:14.352
except I get to help the environment.

00:14:14.352 --> 00:14:16.977
I get to feel like I am
actually contributing

00:14:16.977 --> 00:14:19.229
to the green energy movement.

00:14:19.229 --> 00:14:22.021
- [Host] There are over 4,400
wind turbine technicians

00:14:22.021 --> 00:14:23.502
in the United States

00:14:23.502 --> 00:14:27.196
with jobs expected to
double in the next 10 years.

00:14:27.196 --> 00:14:30.324
- Next time you're passing
a wind turbine, look close.

00:14:30.324 --> 00:14:31.657
Look real close.

00:14:31.657 --> 00:14:33.644
You might see a little speck up there

00:14:33.644 --> 00:14:37.811
and that little speck might
be me living my dream.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:44.466
- [Host] It's our generation's
canary in the coal mine.

00:14:44.466 --> 00:14:46.632
A disorder called colony collapse

00:14:46.632 --> 00:14:50.720
is ravaging our bee population
by one third each year,

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:52.387
endangering our future.

00:14:52.387 --> 00:14:56.554
- The colony collapse disorder
is a mystery to scientists.

00:14:59.062 --> 00:15:01.898
It’s not one thing that's killing them.

00:15:01.898 --> 00:15:05.651
I think it's everything
we are doing in nature.

00:15:05.651 --> 00:15:07.778
- [Host] With nearly one
third of the American diet

00:15:07.778 --> 00:15:10.868
made up of crops pollinated by honeybees,

00:15:10.868 --> 00:15:12.954
bee keepers across the
globe are struggling

00:15:12.954 --> 00:15:14.497
to protect their colonies.

00:15:14.497 --> 00:15:18.288
- [Man] The commercialization
of bee keeping

00:15:18.288 --> 00:15:21.337
is hanging on a very fine thread.

00:15:21.337 --> 00:15:25.795
The bees, if they go,
we are in such trouble.

00:15:25.795 --> 00:15:28.882
- [Host] But in a tiny town
in the Blue Ridge Mountains,

00:15:28.882 --> 00:15:32.554
Gunther Hauk works to change
out relationship with bees

00:15:32.554 --> 00:15:34.725
to ensure our mutual survival.

00:15:34.725 --> 00:15:37.730
- A crisis is always a great blessing

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:40.894
because a crisis always tells us

00:15:40.894 --> 00:15:44.644
that the path we are on
is not the right one.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:52.863
When you look into bee-keeping,

00:15:52.863 --> 00:15:56.534
everything that was invented
in the last 100 years

00:15:56.534 --> 00:15:59.915
was invented for the
bottom line of bee-keeping.

00:15:59.915 --> 00:16:01.582
- The overuse of pesticides,

00:16:01.582 --> 00:16:03.876
the development of monoculture farming

00:16:03.876 --> 00:16:06.336
have all done a pretty hard number

00:16:06.336 --> 00:16:10.503
on the pollinators that we
do rely on for our food.

00:16:12.889 --> 00:16:15.889
- A monoculture is a field of plants

00:16:17.269 --> 00:16:20.111
where only this plant exists.

00:16:20.111 --> 00:16:21.194
Nothing else.

00:16:22.392 --> 00:16:24.898
- [Host] Monoculture
farming is widely used

00:16:24.898 --> 00:16:27.818
to increase agricultural efficiency.

00:16:27.818 --> 00:16:31.418
Over time, it can lead to
accelerated crop infestation

00:16:31.418 --> 00:16:36.073
and disease, requiring
increased use of pesticides.

00:16:36.073 --> 00:16:39.538
Industrialized bees are
shipped thousands of miles

00:16:39.538 --> 00:16:43.705
from crop to crop to pollinate
vast monoculture fields.

00:16:46.627 --> 00:16:51.134
- Bees being trucked from
apples in New York state

00:16:51.134 --> 00:16:54.933
to Maine for the blueberries
and then back down to Florida,

00:16:54.933 --> 00:16:58.724
that's like us eating only
broccoli for one month

00:16:58.724 --> 00:17:02.599
and the next month we
only eat wheat and so on.

00:17:02.599 --> 00:17:05.359
We would not be healthy.

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:07.310
- The commercial bee-keeping world

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:10.898
is not resisting change
because they don't care

00:17:10.898 --> 00:17:12.905
that their bees are having a hard time.

00:17:12.905 --> 00:17:15.950
It's because changing their practices

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:20.077
would make their jobs and
careers unsustainable.

00:17:20.077 --> 00:17:23.165
People like Gunther who
are trying new things

00:17:23.165 --> 00:17:25.334
on a relatively small scale

00:17:25.334 --> 00:17:27.880
are adding valuable perspective

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:30.630
and maybe someday a path forward.

00:17:31.632 --> 00:17:34.590
- [Host] At Gunter Hauk's
honeybee sanctuary,

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:37.344
localized bee-keeping
methods demonstrate hope

00:17:37.344 --> 00:17:38.972
for honeybee health.

00:17:38.972 --> 00:17:40.619
- A honeybee sanctuary.

00:17:40.619 --> 00:17:42.392
It's a contemporary idea.

00:17:42.392 --> 00:17:46.976
Honeybees did not need a
sanctuary until very recently.

00:17:46.976 --> 00:17:48.867
- [Gunther] We work with bees

00:17:48.867 --> 00:17:52.110
in a highly unconventional way.

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:53.358
- [Alex] We don't need to ship our bees

00:17:53.358 --> 00:17:55.319
in order to be successful.

00:17:55.319 --> 00:17:58.402
A localized bee is the strongest bee.

00:18:00.702 --> 00:18:04.202
- We have an apiary here with 35 colonies.

00:18:08.921 --> 00:18:10.504
We are on 25 acres,

00:18:12.468 --> 00:18:15.340
planting things for the bees

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:19.507
so that the bees can forage
on a lot of different plants

00:18:20.762 --> 00:18:24.012
that are good for them and good for us.

00:18:25.226 --> 00:18:27.979
Bees, they are actually sun creatures.

00:18:27.979 --> 00:18:32.273
They don't fly out at
night, only after sunrise.

00:18:32.273 --> 00:18:36.110
And they go home before the sun sets.

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:39.281
Climate change and the warming

00:18:39.281 --> 00:18:42.364
has definitely an effect on the bees.

00:18:43.827 --> 00:18:45.329
- [Host] According to Hauk,

00:18:45.329 --> 00:18:47.122
warmer winters play a role in disrupting

00:18:47.122 --> 00:18:49.207
the rhythms of a healthy hive.

00:18:49.207 --> 00:18:51.413
- On a normal year,

00:18:51.413 --> 00:18:53.839
a hive might need only
10, 15 pounds of honey

00:18:53.839 --> 00:18:55.130
to survive the winter time.

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:57.007
When it's warm in the winter time

00:18:57.007 --> 00:18:59.761
but there's no forage for
them to gather outside,

00:18:59.761 --> 00:19:01.556
they're eating a lot of honey

00:19:01.556 --> 00:19:03.302
but they're not bringing anything else in.

00:19:03.302 --> 00:19:06.853
So you have trouble with starving.

00:19:06.853 --> 00:19:09.438
Many bee-keepers in our area lost

00:19:09.438 --> 00:19:13.311
80% of their colonies last
winter due to this up and down.

00:19:13.311 --> 00:19:16.728
And our losses are only five to ten percent.

00:19:20.027 --> 00:19:23.491
- We can actually leave
all the honey for the bees

00:19:23.491 --> 00:19:26.831
that they need to go through the winter.

00:19:26.831 --> 00:19:28.974
We really only take the surplus.

00:19:28.974 --> 00:19:31.959
We actually cater to their needs,

00:19:31.959 --> 00:19:35.436
and the result is that
they are very healthy.

00:19:35.436 --> 00:19:39.094
There is a natural
harmony in all of creation

00:19:39.094 --> 00:19:41.512
and it is all interrelated.

00:19:41.512 --> 00:19:45.221
The bees can actually
teach us so many things.

00:19:45.221 --> 00:19:49.854
One of the things we can
learn is really to serve

00:19:49.854 --> 00:19:51.437
is better for life.

00:19:52.604 --> 00:19:54.358
It's a full hive.

00:19:54.358 --> 00:19:58.571
We don't rely on asking the
bees what can I get out of you?

00:19:58.571 --> 00:20:00.778
We actually can serve the bees

00:20:00.778 --> 00:20:04.945
and ask the question: what do
you really need to be healthy?

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:16.040
(laughter)

00:20:17.131 --> 00:20:19.256
- I definitely look up to my sister.

00:20:19.256 --> 00:20:22.469
She has a lot of great
things that I can learn from.

00:20:22.469 --> 00:20:25.769
And I think she's an awesome older sister.

00:20:25.769 --> 00:20:27.265
- Ow.

00:20:27.265 --> 00:20:28.641
Thank you, Meimei.

00:20:28.641 --> 00:20:30.352
Kimberly is my best friend.

00:20:30.352 --> 00:20:33.234
She's never gonna be replaced.

00:20:33.234 --> 00:20:36.105
- [Kimberly] I like to do martial arts,

00:20:36.105 --> 00:20:38.900
and then I also like to do basketball.

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:40.817
- I like to do archery.

00:20:42.996 --> 00:20:45.280
And to play piano.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:47.494
I have a lot of favorite subjects.

00:20:47.494 --> 00:20:50.410
I like math and english and social studies

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:52.542
and science and technology.

00:20:52.542 --> 00:20:55.288
- I like technology the best.

00:20:55.288 --> 00:20:57.632
These are some of the programs that I did.

00:20:57.632 --> 00:21:00.007
But I also really like science.

00:21:00.007 --> 00:21:01.674
- I like challenges.

00:21:02.591 --> 00:21:04.885
We saw some videos of people doing

00:21:04.885 --> 00:21:07.602
weather balloon launches online.

00:21:07.602 --> 00:21:09.058
- We thought that it would be a really fun

00:21:09.058 --> 00:21:10.808
family project to do.

00:21:16.449 --> 00:21:18.609
(air hissing)

00:21:18.609 --> 00:21:22.776
A weather balloon is basically
just a very large balloon.

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:24.805
- There is weather.

00:21:24.805 --> 00:21:26.113
- To see what the weather is going to do.

00:21:26.113 --> 00:21:27.825
- So they send up different instruments

00:21:27.825 --> 00:21:30.120
attached to the bottom
of the weather balloons.

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:32.333
- And they'll transmit the data and saying

00:21:32.333 --> 00:21:34.745
oh, it's gonna be partly cloudy.

00:21:34.745 --> 00:21:36.498
- There are 16,000 weather balloons

00:21:36.498 --> 00:21:38.544
launched every day around the world.

00:21:38.544 --> 00:21:39.917
The weather companies,

00:21:39.917 --> 00:21:43.210
the forecasters, only recover 30% of them.

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:44.589
The other 70%.

00:21:44.589 --> 00:21:46.219
- Are still floating in space.

00:21:46.219 --> 00:21:47.091
- No, they're not.

00:21:47.091 --> 00:21:47.924
- Well.

00:21:47.924 --> 00:21:49.013
They're popped.
- They're still somewhere.

00:21:49.013 --> 00:21:50.806
- They're somewhere in the earth.

00:21:50.806 --> 00:21:53.231
- Try not to interrupt me.

00:21:53.231 --> 00:21:55.814
(upbeat music)

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:02.941
- [Kimberly] This is
our Loki Lego Launcher,

00:22:02.941 --> 00:22:06.653
the first spacecraft in the
Yeungstaff Space Program.

00:22:06.653 --> 00:22:09.908
- For the first launch, we
didn't really have a goal.

00:22:09.908 --> 00:22:13.036
- We just wanted to get it off the ground.

00:22:13.036 --> 00:22:16.662
- We didn't really understand
as much science then.

00:22:16.662 --> 00:22:18.586
We were kind of just letting go of it

00:22:18.586 --> 00:22:19.921
and hoping that it went well.

00:22:19.921 --> 00:22:22.335
- The calculations were right.

00:22:22.335 --> 00:22:26.716
We call it the Loki Lego
Launcher because Loki is our cat.

00:22:26.716 --> 00:22:30.016
Our flight computer keeps
track of a number of things,

00:22:30.016 --> 00:22:32.847
including altitude, latitude, longitude,

00:22:32.847 --> 00:22:36.977
speed, temperature, pressure,
voltage, current and power.

00:22:36.977 --> 00:22:39.476
There is a lot of data
that we can learn from.

00:22:39.476 --> 00:22:42.525
- The first launch went to 78,000 feet.

00:22:42.525 --> 00:22:44.614
The images were awesome.

00:22:44.614 --> 00:22:46.698
When we first saw them, we screamed.

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:48.861
It's just the blackness of space.

00:22:48.861 --> 00:22:51.825
And then there's Loki sitting
there in the middle of it,

00:22:51.825 --> 00:22:53.575
right on the horizon.

00:23:01.628 --> 00:23:05.506
The first media that kind
of wrote an article about us

00:23:05.506 --> 00:23:10.055
was a local tech news
website called the Geekwire.

00:23:10.055 --> 00:23:14.893
And then someone from the
Washington Post saw it,

00:23:14.893 --> 00:23:16.557
and so they wrote an article about it.

00:23:16.557 --> 00:23:17.932
And then other people saw it

00:23:17.932 --> 00:23:19.812
and they wrote articles about it.

00:23:19.812 --> 00:23:21.689
- [Man] You're gonna have to
bring the Loki Lego Launcher.

00:23:21.689 --> 00:23:23.831
- (gasps) No!

00:23:23.831 --> 00:23:24.943
No! No way!

00:23:24.943 --> 00:23:26.781
No way, seriously?

00:23:26.781 --> 00:23:28.613
- [Man] You're gonna meet the president.

00:23:28.613 --> 00:23:30.824
- Our parents told us that we were going

00:23:30.824 --> 00:23:32.324
to the White House Science Fair.

00:23:32.324 --> 00:23:34.907
(upbeat music)

00:23:37.579 --> 00:23:40.082
- Well hello everybody,
welcome to the White House.

00:23:40.082 --> 00:23:41.831
- When he actually
walked through the doors,

00:23:41.831 --> 00:23:45.331
we were all in complete awe and like, admiration

00:23:46.435 --> 00:23:49.923
because this is President Obama.

00:23:49.923 --> 00:23:53.387
- He was really easy to talk
to and he was really friendly.

00:23:53.387 --> 00:23:54.809
- [Obama] What do you guys have here?

00:23:54.809 --> 00:23:57.892
- So we built a spacecraft
called the Loki Lego Launcher

00:23:57.892 --> 00:24:00.477
and we sent it up to 78,000 feet.

00:24:00.477 --> 00:24:02.313
- [Obama] That's crazy!

00:24:02.313 --> 00:24:06.152
- He talks to all of
these like famous world leaders

00:24:06.152 --> 00:24:09.780
and he makes decisions that
affect the fate of our country.

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:12.572
And we're just nine and 11-year-old girls

00:24:12.572 --> 00:24:14.739
who did a science project.

00:24:16.535 --> 00:24:18.870
This is a rough run of the flight computer

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:22.370
and we'll probably put the VI sensor here.

00:24:23.291 --> 00:24:26.128
I'm making some changes
to our original design

00:24:26.128 --> 00:24:27.881
because we're adding some new components

00:24:27.881 --> 00:24:29.548
and taking off some.

00:24:35.343 --> 00:24:38.684
- On the second launch,
we added a bigger balloon

00:24:38.684 --> 00:24:41.227
because we wanted it to go higher

00:24:41.227 --> 00:24:44.522
and we also wanted it to go faster.

00:24:44.522 --> 00:24:47.189
We added an APRS radio tracker,

00:24:47.189 --> 00:24:49.900
which sent us location and altitude data

00:24:49.900 --> 00:24:51.530
in almost real time.

00:24:51.530 --> 00:24:53.615
- However, the APRS might not be

00:24:53.615 --> 00:24:55.286
very accurate at ground level.

00:24:55.286 --> 00:24:59.093
So having the GPS unit
as well is very helpful.

00:24:59.093 --> 00:25:03.624
We rewired voltage slash
current slash power sensor, 

00:25:03.624 --> 00:25:05.421
so it would connect to a solar panel

00:25:05.421 --> 00:25:07.297
that we installed on our spacecraft

00:25:07.297 --> 00:25:11.589
to measure how much current
it was getting from the sun.

00:25:11.589 --> 00:25:15.805
And we had a hypothesis that
as our spacecraft went higher,

00:25:15.805 --> 00:25:17.096
we would have more solar current

00:25:17.096 --> 00:25:20.510
because we thought that
since there's less particles

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:24.686
in the air, it wouldn't
block the sun's rays.

00:25:24.686 --> 00:25:27.439
The last thing that we changed
was our Lego minifigure.

00:25:27.439 --> 00:25:30.400
We thought that she would
be a good role model

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:33.530
because she's just a girl
who's strong and she's brave

00:25:33.530 --> 00:25:37.697
and she doesn't give up,
even when things are hard.

00:25:41.077 --> 00:25:43.494
Soon, it was time for launch.

00:25:47.502 --> 00:25:49.585
- [Both] Three, two, one.

00:25:50.505 --> 00:25:52.010
- Lift off!

00:25:52.010 --> 00:25:54.593
(upbeat music)

00:26:01.019 --> 00:26:01.852
Do you see it?

00:26:01.852 --> 00:26:03.974
- [Rebecca] We couldn't
believe that it actually went

00:26:03.974 --> 00:26:05.605
101,000 feet.

00:26:05.605 --> 00:26:06.646
I think we screamed again.

00:26:06.646 --> 00:26:07.479
(screams)

00:26:07.479 --> 00:26:09.481
- [Kimberly] Our current
data was really interesting

00:26:09.481 --> 00:26:12.024
because our solar panel
was producing more current

00:26:12.024 --> 00:26:14.028
as the altitude was going up,

00:26:14.028 --> 00:26:17.280
which indicated that our
hypothesis was correct.

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:19.532
- Getting something correct,

00:26:19.532 --> 00:26:21.450
especially when you don't know
that much about the subject

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:22.950
always feels good.

00:26:26.167 --> 00:26:30.002
I hope that more kids, especially girls,

00:26:30.002 --> 00:26:33.549
follow paths and do
projects like ours and STEM.

00:26:33.549 --> 00:26:36.090
And I hope that our projects

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:38.805
teach them that they can
do whatever they want

00:26:38.805 --> 00:26:40.516
if they put their heart into it.

00:26:40.516 --> 00:26:45.064
- They don't have to stick with
whatever is girly or boyish.

00:26:45.064 --> 00:26:48.352
Girls are awesome and
girls can do anything.

00:26:48.352 --> 00:26:50.602
(laughter)

00:26:57.611 --> 00:26:59.907
- [Host] It's taken 6,000 years for nature

00:26:59.907 --> 00:27:03.324
to carve out these remarkable formations.

00:27:05.616 --> 00:27:07.257
The Marble Caves of Chile Chico

00:27:07.257 --> 00:27:10.460
are located on a peninsula of solid marble

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:12.295
bordering a remote glacial lake

00:27:12.295 --> 00:27:15.795
that spans the Chile and Argentina border.

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:19.056
The lake is fed by rivers

00:27:19.056 --> 00:27:22.473
coming from several surrounding glaciers.

00:27:22.473 --> 00:27:24.847
As the glaciers melt and grow,

00:27:24.847 --> 00:27:27.435
the water slowly carves the marble,

00:27:27.435 --> 00:27:31.359
leaving behind masterpieces
fit for a museum.

00:27:31.359 --> 00:27:34.021
The distinct bright blue waters are caused

00:27:34.021 --> 00:27:36.865
by suspended particles from the glaciers

00:27:36.865 --> 00:27:39.863
reflecting the blue part of the sunlight.

00:27:39.863 --> 00:27:42.491
Getting to this location isn't easy.

00:27:42.491 --> 00:27:45.372
It's a long journey requiring a flight,

00:27:45.372 --> 00:27:48.372
a 200 mile drive into the wilderness,

00:27:48.372 --> 00:27:50.833
and a boat ride just to get there.

00:27:50.833 --> 00:27:52.044
But it's worth it.

00:27:52.044 --> 00:27:55.168
The remarkable swirling
patterns on the blue cave

00:27:55.168 --> 00:27:57.257
reflect the lake's blue waters,

00:27:57.257 --> 00:28:01.174
changing with the water
levels and the seasons.

00:28:04.932 --> 00:28:07.541
Nature truly is the greatest artist.

00:28:07.541 --> 00:28:09.958
(calm music)

00:28:16.803 --> 00:28:18.114
- Don't go anywhere.

00:28:18.114 --> 00:28:20.239
Being in a bald eagle nest is surreal.

00:28:20.239 --> 00:28:21.958
This is a wild animal.

00:28:21.958 --> 00:28:23.496
She's being rowdy.

00:28:23.496 --> 00:28:26.328
It's very capable of hurting
you if you're not careful,

00:28:26.328 --> 00:28:28.039
so you have to take your time.

00:28:28.039 --> 00:28:29.206
A little rough, huh?

00:28:29.206 --> 00:28:30.252
Sorry about that.

00:28:30.252 --> 00:28:33.835
It's an experience
that's hard to describe.

00:28:35.875 --> 00:28:37.716
My name is Jim Campbell-Spickler.

00:28:37.716 --> 00:28:41.925
I'm a forest canopy ecologist
and a wildlife biologist.

00:28:41.925 --> 00:28:46.092
And I'm pretty sure I have
the best job in the world.

00:28:47.892 --> 00:28:49.645
(bird whistles)

00:28:49.645 --> 00:28:52.773
My job is to climb into the eagle's nest

00:28:52.773 --> 00:28:55.564
and capture and work with chicks.

00:28:55.564 --> 00:28:56.733
Yeah, he's a monster.

00:28:56.733 --> 00:28:58.193
And the reason why were doing this

00:28:58.193 --> 00:29:00.914
is we're trying to get blood samples

00:29:00.914 --> 00:29:03.286
and we can use those blood samples

00:29:03.286 --> 00:29:06.283
to look at environmental contaminants

00:29:06.283 --> 00:29:09.790
and how they get into the
bald eagle's food source.

00:29:09.790 --> 00:29:11.122
You're gonna make me chase you, huh?

00:29:11.122 --> 00:29:12.254
Why don't you come off of there?

00:29:12.254 --> 00:29:13.173
Come down.

00:29:13.173 --> 00:29:15.507
It's a challenging species to work with.

00:29:15.507 --> 00:29:18.378
It's got a really sharp hooked beak

00:29:18.378 --> 00:29:21.636
and its talons are big and powerful.

00:29:21.636 --> 00:29:23.763
And you don't want to let
them grab a hold of you

00:29:23.763 --> 00:29:25.633
because they can really do some damage.

00:29:25.633 --> 00:29:27.716
All right, let's go down.

00:29:29.061 --> 00:29:30.186
This is a wild animal,

00:29:30.186 --> 00:29:33.186
so you never know what could happen.

00:29:36.483 --> 00:29:39.027
The Channel Islands are an island chain

00:29:39.027 --> 00:29:41.149
off the coast of southern California.

00:29:41.149 --> 00:29:42.651
These guys don't look too bad.

00:29:42.651 --> 00:29:44.362
They're a desert island system.

00:29:44.362 --> 00:29:47.205
You get out there and you can
stand on top of these bluffs

00:29:47.205 --> 00:29:48.665
and you can see the Pacific.

00:29:48.665 --> 00:29:52.998
A whole 360 degree view of
just the most beautiful ocean

00:29:52.998 --> 00:29:55.747
you could ever imagine.

00:29:55.747 --> 00:29:58.048
You've got not only bald eagles.

00:29:58.048 --> 00:29:59.757
You have peregrine falcons

00:29:59.757 --> 00:30:01.631
that are nesting on the sea cliffs.

00:30:01.631 --> 00:30:03.345
Just tell me if she's coming in.

00:30:03.345 --> 00:30:04.847
Oh!

00:30:04.847 --> 00:30:08.222
You've got a host of different sea birds.

00:30:08.222 --> 00:30:10.722
And it's just a magical place.

00:30:12.765 --> 00:30:15.110
Our research, it may look invasive.

00:30:15.110 --> 00:30:17.774
But really, one of our primary concerns

00:30:17.774 --> 00:30:21.480
is the safety of these young
birds that we're handling.

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:23.363
So we go to great lengths to ensure

00:30:23.363 --> 00:30:25.206
that we don't injure them

00:30:25.206 --> 00:30:27.742
and that when we put
them back in the nest,

00:30:27.742 --> 00:30:32.287
they're just as well off as
when we first encountered them.

00:30:32.287 --> 00:30:33.370
He likes you.

00:30:34.210 --> 00:30:35.827
The bald eagle's our national bird,

00:30:35.827 --> 00:30:37.089
so it's quite an honor for me

00:30:37.089 --> 00:30:39.127
to get to work with this species.

00:30:39.127 --> 00:30:42.169
Quite often, I'll just sit
in that nest for a moment

00:30:42.169 --> 00:30:44.963
and just take in the
view and just think, wow.

00:30:44.963 --> 00:30:46.756
It's just an honor to get up there

00:30:46.756 --> 00:30:49.302
and be able to share that
view and that experience

00:30:49.302 --> 00:30:52.552
with them just for that moment in time.

00:31:01.523 --> 00:31:04.356
- Atropa belladonna will kill you.

00:31:06.554 --> 00:31:09.904
Datura will put you to sleep forever.

00:31:09.904 --> 00:31:11.821
Aconitum will kill you.

00:31:12.967 --> 00:31:16.300
Laung will produce cyanide and kill you.

00:31:22.208 --> 00:31:24.423
Every plant here in the
Poison Garden is poisonous

00:31:24.423 --> 00:31:27.090
and has the ability to kill you.

00:31:29.182 --> 00:31:30.511
My name's Trevor Jones.

00:31:30.511 --> 00:31:32.863
I'm the head gardener of Alnwick Garden.

00:31:32.863 --> 00:31:35.017
This plant is giant hogweed.

00:31:35.017 --> 00:31:37.435
It will get up to around
about eight foot high.

00:31:37.435 --> 00:31:39.892
It's phototoxic so will burn your skin

00:31:39.892 --> 00:31:42.983
and give you blisters
for up to seven years.

00:31:42.983 --> 00:31:45.105
This garden is set in the Wall Garden

00:31:45.105 --> 00:31:48.277
of the Old Castle in
Northumberland in the UK.

00:31:48.277 --> 00:31:51.075
We would have around about 95 plants.

00:31:51.075 --> 00:31:53.659
And we're adding to the
collection all the time.

00:31:53.659 --> 00:31:56.162
This plant is aconitum or monkshood.

00:31:56.162 --> 00:31:57.584
A wonderful brood of flowers,

00:31:57.584 --> 00:31:59.517
but the whole of the plant is poisonous.

00:31:59.517 --> 00:32:02.754
The berries crushed up and
fed to you will kill you.

00:32:02.754 --> 00:32:05.048
The leaves themselves will kill you also,

00:32:05.048 --> 00:32:07.047
as will the root and the stem.

00:32:07.047 --> 00:32:08.529
We have to obviously
maintain the garden,

00:32:08.529 --> 00:32:10.387
so we have to tend the plants.

00:32:10.387 --> 00:32:11.384
And when we do that,

00:32:11.384 --> 00:32:13.973
we have to be very careful
of the way we operate.

00:32:13.973 --> 00:32:16.058
So we have to cover some of our skin

00:32:16.058 --> 00:32:19.936
when we deal with
particularly dangerous plants.

00:32:19.936 --> 00:32:21.312
This plant is laung.

00:32:21.312 --> 00:32:22.895
It produces cyanide.

00:32:22.895 --> 00:32:24.897
And we all know what that'll do to you.

00:32:24.897 --> 00:32:26.569
So it was the charge of the duchess,

00:32:26.569 --> 00:32:27.940
Duchess of Northumberland.

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:29.868
So rather than having a herb garden,

00:32:29.868 --> 00:32:31.911
she decided to create more interest

00:32:31.911 --> 00:32:33.326
and have a poison garden.

00:32:33.326 --> 00:32:34.786
Well they're very, very common plants.

00:32:34.786 --> 00:32:36.325
In fact, a lot of them are what we call

00:32:36.325 --> 00:32:37.701
cottage garden plants.

00:32:37.701 --> 00:32:39.282
And they're grown in
many people's gardens,

00:32:39.282 --> 00:32:43.874
but people don't know how
harmful they actually are.

00:32:43.874 --> 00:32:45.542
This is atropa belladonna.

00:32:45.542 --> 00:32:48.760
Four berries are enough to kill a child.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:51.134
People are intrigued by poisonous plants.

00:32:51.134 --> 00:32:52.595
I'm often very worried when they come out

00:32:52.595 --> 00:32:55.137
because many of them will be
growing these plants at home.

00:32:55.137 --> 00:32:57.597
They don't realize the
powerful impact plants can have

00:32:57.597 --> 00:32:58.930
on us as humans.

00:32:59.810 --> 00:33:01.932
- [Woman] Is it something
that you find fascinating?

00:33:01.932 --> 00:33:02.891
- Definitely.

00:33:02.891 --> 00:33:04.099
- [Woman] Why?

00:33:04.099 --> 00:33:06.816
- It's a good way to get rid of your wife.

00:33:06.816 --> 00:33:09.066
(laughter)

00:33:15.544 --> 00:33:17.498
- [Host] In this curious lagoon,

00:33:17.498 --> 00:33:20.908
the rivers may run red, but rest assured,

00:33:20.908 --> 00:33:22.741
it's water, not blood.

00:33:23.623 --> 00:33:27.790
Although you could be forgiven
for confusing the two.

00:33:30.548 --> 00:33:33.930
The Red Lagoon is located
12,000 feet above sea level

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:36.678
in an area of Chile so remote

00:33:36.678 --> 00:33:39.853
that it wasn't even known to
the National Tourism Office

00:33:39.853 --> 00:33:40.770
until 2009.

00:33:42.227 --> 00:33:43.895
And maybe that's a good thing

00:33:43.895 --> 00:33:46.312
because much like the pyramids in Egypt,

00:33:46.312 --> 00:33:49.399
the lagoon is believed to be cursed.

00:33:49.399 --> 00:33:53.566
Legend says that the waters
are owned by the devil himself.

00:33:56.281 --> 00:33:57.951
The thermal temperature of the water

00:33:57.951 --> 00:33:59.701
at 100 to 120 degrees

00:34:00.828 --> 00:34:04.248
stops even wild animals
from approaching it.

00:34:04.248 --> 00:34:08.173
Devils aside, what causes
this blood red water?

00:34:08.173 --> 00:34:10.795
Well, biologists studying the lagoon

00:34:10.795 --> 00:34:12.573
recently traced it to algae

00:34:12.573 --> 00:34:14.388
growing in the depths of the lake.

00:34:14.388 --> 00:34:17.931
Still, much of this
lagoon remains a mystery.

00:34:17.931 --> 00:34:20.180
A visit to this place is a reminder

00:34:20.180 --> 00:34:24.347
that there's still much to
marvel at on this planet.

00:34:29.276 --> 00:34:32.072
- I've had an absolute
fascination with sharks

00:34:32.072 --> 00:34:34.155
since I was a little kid.

00:34:37.031 --> 00:34:38.655
I knew I wanted to be a marine biologist

00:34:38.655 --> 00:34:40.407
by the time I was five,

00:34:40.407 --> 00:34:42.286
and I got to snorkel with my first shark

00:34:42.286 --> 00:34:44.080
when I was about eight years old.

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:48.875
And I was literally hooked
on these awesome animals.

00:34:48.875 --> 00:34:50.002
I'm Jillian Morris.

00:34:50.002 --> 00:34:52.963
I am a shark conservationist,
a shark diver,

00:34:52.963 --> 00:34:55.546
and an underwater videographer.

00:34:56.972 --> 00:34:58.804
I've traveled all over the world

00:34:58.804 --> 00:35:00.015
and I have to say that Bimini

00:35:00.015 --> 00:35:02.685
has some of the bluest
waters I've ever seen.

00:35:02.685 --> 00:35:03.850
All different shades.

00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:07.397
Some people joke about the
50 shades of blue in Bimini.

00:35:07.397 --> 00:35:09.194
The island is really, really small.

00:35:09.194 --> 00:35:10.897
It's about six miles long.

00:35:10.897 --> 00:35:13.526
But, the world just below the surface

00:35:13.526 --> 00:35:15.527
is really what's incredible.

00:35:15.527 --> 00:35:17.198
You've got blue crystal clear water.

00:35:17.198 --> 00:35:19.032
It's teeming with marine life

00:35:19.032 --> 00:35:23.199
and is the reason why we live
on this tiny little island.

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:25.870
The great hammerhead dive site

00:35:25.870 --> 00:35:27.705
is about a quarter of a mile off shore.

00:35:27.705 --> 00:35:30.171
It's a place where in very shallow water,

00:35:30.171 --> 00:35:33.547
you're able to get in and
see these very large sharks

00:35:33.547 --> 00:35:35.380
up close and personal.

00:35:36.264 --> 00:35:39.089
It's very rare to actually
be able to be in the water

00:35:39.089 --> 00:35:41.688
with so many of them like
we can here in Bimini.

00:35:41.688 --> 00:35:45.601
Especially in crystal clear shallow water.

00:35:45.601 --> 00:35:49.063
Unfortunately, sharks get
a really bad reputation.

00:35:49.063 --> 00:35:50.731
They're not man eating monsters.

00:35:50.731 --> 00:35:53.111
They're not mindless eating machines.

00:35:53.111 --> 00:35:54.984
They're actually really intelligent,

00:35:54.984 --> 00:35:57.362
social, incredible animals.

00:35:57.362 --> 00:36:00.067
They're also really incredible
for the health of our oceans,

00:36:00.067 --> 00:36:03.951
which all of us rely on every single day.

00:36:03.951 --> 00:36:06.747
There's something really
special about hammerheads.

00:36:06.747 --> 00:36:08.785
I've been in the water
with some very big sharks,

00:36:08.785 --> 00:36:11.083
including great whites and tiger sharks,

00:36:11.083 --> 00:36:13.752
but nothing compares to
the great hammerhead.

00:36:13.752 --> 00:36:17.421
The way they move, their
power, their grace.

00:36:17.421 --> 00:36:21.969
They can turn on a dime and
they have those wide set eyes.

00:36:21.969 --> 00:36:24.010
They're my favorite animal on the planet

00:36:24.010 --> 00:36:26.526
and being able to dive
with them as much as we do,

00:36:26.526 --> 00:36:28.859
I feel really, really lucky.

00:36:33.944 --> 00:36:36.148
(birds calling)

00:36:36.148 --> 00:36:40.065
(speaking in foreign language)

00:36:51.124 --> 00:36:52.569
- [Host] In Chennai, India,

00:36:52.569 --> 00:36:54.336
there's a tiny camera repair shop

00:36:54.336 --> 00:36:57.092
run by a man named Joseph Sekar.

00:36:57.092 --> 00:37:01.009
(speaking in foreign language)

00:37:02.886 --> 00:37:05.597
Sekar got his nickname because every day

00:37:05.597 --> 00:37:07.977
he feeds thousands of hungry parakeets

00:37:07.977 --> 00:37:09.100
on the roof of his home.

00:37:09.100 --> 00:37:13.017
(speaking in foreign language)

00:37:21.571 --> 00:37:24.573
The 2006 tsunami that hit southeast Asia

00:37:24.573 --> 00:37:28.574
flooded parts of India and
displaced thousands of birds.

00:37:28.574 --> 00:37:31.829
Sekar noticed a pair of
parakeets outside his home

00:37:31.829 --> 00:37:33.911
and set some rice out for them.

00:37:33.911 --> 00:37:36.419
Then more started to show up and well.

00:37:36.419 --> 00:37:40.336
(speaking in foreign language)

00:37:43.884 --> 00:37:48.179
Feeding 8,000 parakeets twice
a day is a great expense.

00:37:48.179 --> 00:37:50.974
Sekar spends about 40%
of the money he makes

00:37:50.974 --> 00:37:54.558
repairing cameras to
buy food for the birds.

00:37:54.558 --> 00:37:58.475
(speaking in foreign language)

00:37:59.398 --> 00:38:01.302
But for the Birdman of Chennai,

00:38:01.302 --> 00:38:03.399
there's more to life than money.

00:38:03.399 --> 00:38:07.066
(speaking foreign language)

00:38:32.974 --> 00:38:35.641
(birds calling)

00:38:50.448 --> 00:38:52.170
- Darkness is the canvas.

00:38:52.170 --> 00:38:53.914
The light is my brush.

00:38:53.914 --> 00:38:54.954
With light painting,

00:38:54.954 --> 00:38:58.454
you can create new worlds in the darkness.

00:39:01.417 --> 00:39:05.417
My name is Hannu Huhtamo,
and I paint with light.

00:39:11.385 --> 00:39:13.264
I live in Finland.

00:39:13.264 --> 00:39:16.231
We have amazing nature here.

00:39:16.231 --> 00:39:21.148
We have long summer nights that
are warm and full of light.

00:39:21.148 --> 00:39:25.315
But on the opposite, there
is also this dark part.

00:39:26.607 --> 00:39:28.992
It's called the polar night.

00:39:28.992 --> 00:39:31.492
It starts in December,

00:39:31.492 --> 00:39:34.075
and it lasts for about 50 days.

00:39:35.121 --> 00:39:38.954
The sun doesn't rise
above the horizon at all.

00:39:39.996 --> 00:39:43.663
And for a light painter,
that is quite good.

00:39:45.669 --> 00:39:47.805
I do long exposure photography,

00:39:47.805 --> 00:39:50.803
which is a photographic technique

00:39:50.803 --> 00:39:53.636
that allows me to draw with light.

00:39:54.598 --> 00:39:58.765
The exposure times can vary
from, uh, seconds to hours.

00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:02.064
And the idea is to use the light source

00:40:02.064 --> 00:40:04.231
as the brushes or pencils.

00:40:05.318 --> 00:40:08.735
And the dark surroundings are the canvas.

00:40:10.778 --> 00:40:15.451
When I start, I might do
some sketches on paper.

00:40:15.451 --> 00:40:18.201
After that, I go to the location.

00:40:19.417 --> 00:40:23.883
I search for details
that I want to emphasize.

00:40:23.883 --> 00:40:25.253
In some light treatment,

00:40:25.253 --> 00:40:29.587
it will start to look like
a completely new place.

00:40:29.587 --> 00:40:33.885
Going to a dark woods or
to an abandoned house,

00:40:33.885 --> 00:40:36.429
you first think that oh,
I don't want to go there

00:40:36.429 --> 00:40:39.807
because they're just so
nasty-looking dark places.

00:40:39.807 --> 00:40:40.975
But through light painting,

00:40:40.975 --> 00:40:45.142
I have examined these places
and they look beautiful.

00:40:47.899 --> 00:40:50.149
See the place differently.

